{"name":"Pond’s Edge","hex":"#b6c9b8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pond","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An inland body of standing water, either natural or man-made, that is smaller than a lake.","An inland body of standing water of any size that is fed by springs rather than by a river."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Chiefly in across the pond: the Atlantic Ocean."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pond"},{"term":"edge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The boundary line of a surface.","A one-dimensional face of a polytope. In particular, the joining line between two vertices of a polygon; the place where two faces of a polyhedron meet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move an object slowly and carefully in a particular direction.","To move slowly and carefully in a particular direction."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/edge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pond","blocks":[{"text":"A pond is a small, still, land-based body of water formed by pooling inside a depression, either naturally or artificially. A pond is smaller than a lake and there are no official criteria distinguishing the two, although defining a pond to be less than 5 hectares in area, less than 5 metres (16 ft) in depth and with less than 30% of its area covered by emergent vegetation helps in distinguishing the ecology of ponds from those of lakes and wetlands."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Cornjum%2C_Martenastate_010.jpg/330px-Cornjum%2C_Martenastate_010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pond","wikidata":"Q12719646"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Early_Pond.jpg/500px-Early_Pond.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Early_Pond.jpg/500px-Early_